From: Loong <1031313955@qq.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: add the web recovery server (Airoha Web U-Boot)

The board's documented recovery path is TFTP plus the bootmenu: it needs a
serial console and a TFTP server on the other machine.  Next to a bricked
router there is usually just a cable and a browser.

This serves a recovery page on port 80, takes images over
multipart/form-data, and hands each one to the board's existing environment
scripts (or a built-in equivalent when the saved environment predates
them).  One sidebar entry per task:

  - routine flash: the sysupgrade firmware into fit;
  - bootloader: BL2 and/or the U-Boot FIP, optionally with the firmware and
    a rebuild of the ubi partition for a first migration.  A routine FIP
    update writes fip in place and leaves rootfs_data alone;
  - back to stock (CONFIG_CMD_HTTPD_STOCK_RESTORE, per board): an image
    written to the bare flash at an erase-block offset, offset 0 being the
    whole chip;
  - UBI volumes: the per-unit factory data volumes named in
    CONFIG_HTTPD_FACTORY_VOLS, checked for exact size, or any volume by
    name -- created for the file if it does not exist.  "Stay" keeps the
    server up afterwards so several can be restored in one sitting;
  - device details: model, SoC, RAM, flash geometry, partitions, MAC,
    U-Boot version and the UBI volume table, all read at request time
    (GET /info).  The page carries no board name, so one copy of it serves
    every board that enables it;
  - about.

Everything that can be checked before the upload is answered -- factory
volume sizes, the raw-write offset and bound, the volume name -- is checked
then, so a refused upload is refused while the browser is still listening.

Built on U-Boot's own TCP stack, modelled on net/fastboot_tcp.c.  tcboot,
the third-party bootloader for this device, embeds uIP 0.9 for the same job
because the U-Boot it forked from had no TCP; that costs ~4000 lines and a
second IP stack competing for the NIC.  Here tftpboot / dhcp / bootmenu keep
working untouched, and CONFIG_CMD_HTTPD defaults to n.

Notable points:

  - rx() receives a stream offset, so the upload lands directly at $loadaddr
    and a 235 MB image needs no second copy in RAM.  Parts are flashed where
    they landed, aligned down to 64 bytes first -- the bytes stepped on
    belong to that part's own multipart header, never to the previous part's
    payload.

  - Requests are classified in on_rcv_nxt_update() rather than rx(), because
    the first segment can be shorter than the request line.

  - A minimal DHCP server hands out one fixed lease.  Without it the user has
    to configure a static address before the page is reachable, which is
    exactly the kind of step that makes a rescue flow fail.

  - The panel LEDs chase while waiting and blink together while writing.  A
    solid panel is indistinguishable from a hung board.  Flashing is
    synchronous, so the blink is driven by the cyclic framework: the SPI-NAND
    layer calls schedule() once per written page.

  - The page is kept as files/httpd/page.html next to this package and
    spliced into net/httpd.c by files/httpd/gen.py, one string literal per
    source line.  Edit the HTML and rerun the script.

diff --git a/include/net-legacy.h b/include/net-legacy.h
--- a/include/net-legacy.h
+++ b/include/net-legacy.h
@@ -303,9 +303,12 @@ extern int		net_restart_wrap;	/* Tried all network devices */
 enum proto_t {
 	BOOTP, RARP, ARP, TFTPGET, DHCP, DHCP6, PING, PING6, DNS, NFS, CDP,
 	NETCONS, SNTP, TFTPSRV, TFTPPUT, LINKLOCAL, FASTBOOT_UDP, FASTBOOT_TCP,
-	WOL, UDP, NCSI, WGET, RS
+	WOL, UDP, NCSI, WGET, RS, HTTPD
 };
 
+/* net/httpd.c */
+void httpd_start_server(void);
+
 /* Indicates whether the file name was specified on the command line */
 extern bool	net_boot_file_name_explicit;
 
diff --git a/net/Kconfig b/net/Kconfig
--- a/net/Kconfig
+++ b/net/Kconfig
@@ -212,6 +212,53 @@ config PROT_TCP
 	  Enable a generic tcp framework that allows defining a custom
 	  handler for tcp protocol.
 
+config CMD_HTTPD
+	bool "httpd - web recovery server"
+	depends on PROT_TCP && CMD_UBI && CMD_MTD
+	help
+	  Start a web recovery server on port 80: one page from which a
+	  browser can flash the firmware, the bootloader, UBI volumes and --
+	  where a board opts in below -- a factory image.  Built on U-Boot's
+	  own TCP stack; no third-party IP stack is required.
+
+	  Nothing board-specific is compiled into the page.  What it shows
+	  about the board is read at request time from the device tree, MTD
+	  and UBI.
+
+config HTTPD_FACTORY_VOLS
+	string "httpd - per-unit factory data volumes"
+	depends on CMD_HTTPD
+	default ""
+	help
+	  UBI volumes holding per-unit factory data that the page offers to
+	  restore from a backup, as space-separated "name:size" pairs with
+	  the size in hex, e.g. "ri:0x40000 bosa:0x40000".  A file is only
+	  written when its length matches the size exactly.  Leave empty on
+	  a board that has no such volumes.
+
+config HTTPD_FACTORY_MAC
+	string "httpd - where the factory MAC lives"
+	depends on CMD_HTTPD
+	default ""
+	help
+	  The UBI volume and hex offset holding the factory MAC address, as
+	  "volume:offset", e.g. "ri:0x3e".  The health check reads it and
+	  reports whether it is a valid address and whether it is the one in
+	  use.  Leave empty on a board whose MAC does not come from a volume.
+
+config CMD_HTTPD_STOCK_RESTORE
+	bool "httpd - raw flash write (back to stock)"
+	depends on CMD_HTTPD
+	help
+	  Add a "back to stock" page able to write an image to the bare
+	  flash device at an erase-block offset -- offset 0 with a full dump
+	  puts the factory firmware back and removes this U-Boot with it.
+
+	  The page checks only that the write fits the chip.  It cannot
+	  tell one model's dump from another's, and a wrong image leaves
+	  nothing bootable -- on the one path meant for a board that is
+	  already broken.  So it is off by default and turned on per board.
+
 config PROT_TCP_SACK
 	bool "TCP SACK support"
 	depends on PROT_TCP
diff --git a/net/Makefile b/net/Makefile
--- a/net/Makefile
+++ b/net/Makefile
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@ obj-$(CONFIG_CMD_SNTP) += sntp.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_CMD_TFTPBOOT) += tftp.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_$(PHASE_)UDP_FUNCTION_FASTBOOT)  += fastboot_udp.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_$(PHASE_)TCP_FUNCTION_FASTBOOT)  += fastboot_tcp.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_CMD_HTTPD) += httpd.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_CMD_WOL)  += wol.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PROT_UDP) += udp.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_PROT_TCP) += tcp.o
diff --git a/net/httpd.c b/net/httpd.c
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/net/httpd.c
@@ -0,0 +1,2602 @@
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0+
+/*
+ * Airoha Web U-Boot -- HTTP recovery server.
+ *
+ * Serves a recovery page on GET and accepts one or more images via
+ * multipart/form-data on POST.  Each part is flashed where the TCP stream
+ * left it, and the flashing itself is delegated to the board's environment
+ * scripts (with a built-in fallback for each), so no flash logic is
+ * duplicated here.
+ *
+ * What the page offers, one task per sidebar entry:
+ *
+ *   routine flash    "firmware" into the fit volume
+ *   bootloader       "bl2" and/or "fip", optionally with the firmware and
+ *                    a rebuild of the whole ubi partition ("format")
+ *   back to stock    "stock" written to raw flash at "stockoff"
+ *                    (CONFIG_CMD_HTTPD_STOCK_RESTORE)
+ *   UBI volumes      per-unit factory data volumes named in
+ *                    CONFIG_HTTPD_FACTORY_VOLS ("fvol_<name>"), or any
+ *                    volume by name ("ubivol" + "ubifile"), created when it
+ *                    does not exist yet; "stay" keeps the server up after
+ *                    the write instead of rebooting
+ *   device details   GET /info, JSON read at request time from the device
+ *                    tree, MTD and UBI -- the page itself carries no board
+ *                    name, so one build of it serves every board
+ *   health check     GET /check (BL2, bad blocks, the UBI volumes the boot
+ *                    needs, factory volumes, U-Boot MAC)
+ *   serial log       GET /log, the console output recorded since power-on
+ *                    (CONFIG_CONSOLE_RECORD)
+ *
+ * The page lives in files/httpd/page.html next to this package; gen.py
+ * splices it in between the PAGE_BEGIN / PAGE_END markers below.  Edit the
+ * HTML, rerun the script, never the string literal by hand.
+ *
+ * Built on U-Boot's own TCP stack (net/tcp.c), modelled on net/fastboot_tcp.c.
+ * No third-party IP stack is pulled in, so tftpboot / dhcp / bootmenu keep
+ * working unchanged.
+ */
+
+#include <command.h>
+#include <console.h>
+#include <cyclic.h>
+#include <dm.h>
+#include <env.h>
+#include <led.h>
+#include <mtd.h>
+#include <net.h>
+#include <net/tcp.h>
+#include <stdarg.h>
+#include <ubi_uboot.h>
+#include <linux/kernel.h>
+#include <linux/err.h>
+#include <linux/libfdt.h>
+#include <linux/mtd/mtd.h>
+#include <membuf.h>
+#include <linux/string.h>
+#include <asm/global_data.h>
+#include <asm/unaligned.h>
+#include <version_string.h>
+#include <vsprintf.h>
+#include "../drivers/mtd/ubi/ubi.h"
+
+DECLARE_GLOBAL_DATA_PTR;
+
+#define HTTPD_PORT		80
+
+/* Version of the recovery page itself, shown in the sidebar and on the console. */
+#define WEB_VERSION		"0.3.0"
+
+#define PROJECT_HOST		"github.com/Loong1996/ImmortalWrt-Airoha"
+#define PROJECT_URL		"https://" PROJECT_HOST
+#define PORTAL_HOST		"loong1996.github.io/ImmortalWrt-Airoha"
+#define PORTAL_URL		"https://" PORTAL_HOST "/"
+
+/* Enough for the request line + headers of any sane browser. */
+#define HDRBUF_SZ		2048
+
+/* tcp->priv values: per-connection classification. */
+#define CONN_UNKNOWN		((void *)0)
+#define CONN_GET		((void *)1)
+#define CONN_POST		((void *)2)
+#define CONN_INFO		((void *)3)
+#define CONN_CHECK		((void *)4)
+#define CONN_LOG		((void *)5)
+
+/*
+ * Commands run after the response has been flushed and net_loop() returned.
+ * Keeping them in the environment means a user can inspect and override the
+ * flashing steps from the U-Boot console without rebuilding.
+ *
+ * Each one has a built-in equivalent, because the saved environment is itself
+ * something recovery has to survive: a board carrying an older ubootenv
+ * volume shadows the defaults compiled into this image, and would fail at the
+ * first step -- precisely when there is no other way in.
+ */
+#define ENV_WRITE_BL2		"httpd_write_bl2"
+#define ENV_FORMAT_UBI		"httpd_format_ubi"
+#define ENV_WRITE_FIP		"httpd_write_fip"
+#define ENV_WRITE_FIT		"ubi_write_production"
+
+#define UBI_PART		"ubi"
+#define CMD_ATTACH_UBI		"ubi part " UBI_PART
+
+#define DEF_WRITE_BL2		"mtd erase bl2 && " \
+				"mtd write bl2 $loadaddr 0x800 $filesize"
+#define DEF_FORMAT_UBI		"ubi detach ; mtd erase " UBI_PART " && " \
+				CMD_ATTACH_UBI
+/*
+ * A routine bootloader update must not touch rootfs_data (the overlay
+ * holding every OpenWrt setting), and does not need to: fip is always
+ * created at the same fixed 0x100000, so an in-place "ubi write" on an
+ * already-existing fip fits inside its own current reservation with
+ * nothing to free.
+ *
+ * ubi_write_fip -- the board's own script, used by the bootmenu entry
+ * immediately followed by reset_factory -- is only reached here when fip
+ * does not exist yet (first migration, before rootfs_data exists either).
+ * That is the one case where its unconditional "evict rootfs_data, then
+ * create" is both necessary and harmless, so it is reused rather than
+ * duplicated.
+ */
+#define DEF_WRITE_FIP		"if ubi check fip ; then " \
+				"ubi write $loadaddr fip $filesize ; " \
+				"else run ubi_write_fip ; fi"
+#define DEF_WRITE_FIT		"ubi check fit && ubi remove fit ; " \
+				"ubi check rootfs_data && ubi remove rootfs_data ; " \
+				"ubi create fit $filesize dynamic && " \
+				"ubi write $loadaddr fit $filesize"
+
+/* UBI's own limit is 127; this bounds the on-stack copies. */
+#define UBIVOL_NAME_MAX		64
+
+/* Form field names. */
+#define FIELD_BL2		"bl2"
+#define FIELD_FIP		"fip"
+#define FIELD_FIT		"firmware"
+#define FIELD_FORMAT		"format"
+#define FIELD_STOCK		"stock"
+#define FIELD_STOCK_OFF		"stockoff"
+#define FIELD_FVOL_PREFIX	"fvol_"
+#define FIELD_UBIVOL_NAME	"ubivol"
+#define FIELD_UBIVOL_FILE	"ubifile"
+#define FIELD_STAY		"stay"
+
+#define MAX_PARTS		12
+#define MAX_FVOLS		8
+
+/*
+ * Parts are flashed in place, so their start address is wherever the form
+ * data happened to land.  Align it down for the NAND layer; a multipart part
+ * header is never shorter than ~90 bytes, so the bytes stepped on belong to
+ * that header and never to the previous part's payload.
+ */
+#define FLASH_ALIGN		64
+
